.HA A "Proof of Theorem 1"
.LP
Let $U sub i \(mo "{" 0,1 "}"$ indicate that $k$-mer $i$ of $A$ is shared, let $U = sum U sub i = n C sub M$, and
let $S sub i ~ \(ap ~ roman Bernoulli (s)$ indicate selection. The $S sub i$ are independent of each other and of
the mutation process $M$, because selection depends only on the hash of $A$'s own $k$-mer.
Let $F sub i ~ \(ap ~ roman Bernoulli ( eps )$ be independent false-positive indicators. Then
$N = sum S sub i$ and $Y = sum S sub i [ U sub i + (1 - U sub i ) F sub i ]$.
.LP
\f[I]Step 1 (conditional on $M$ and $N$).\f[R] Given $N$, the selected set is a uniformly random $N$-subset, so
$U sub S = sum S sub i U sub i$ is hypergeometric:
.DS
	$Ex [ U sub S / N ] = C sub M ,~~~~~~ Var ( U sub S / N ) = C sub M (1 - C sub M ) { n - N } over { N (n-1) } .$	(A1)
.DE
Given $U sub S$, the false-positive count is $roman Bin (N - U sub S , eps )$. Hence
$Ex [ Y/N | M,N ] = C sub M (1- eps ) + eps$, and by the law of total variance within this step,
.DS
	$Var ( Y/N | M,N ) = (1- eps ) sup 2 C sub M (1 - C sub M ) { n - N } over { N (n-1) } + (1 - C sub M ) { eps (1 - eps ) } over N .$	(A2)
.DE
Applying the affine map (5) gives
.DS
	$Ex [ Chat | M, N ] = C sub M ,~~~~~~ Var ( Chat | M, N ) = C sub M (1 - C sub M ) { n - N } over { N(n-1) } + { (1 - C sub M ) eps } over { (1- eps ) N } .$	(A2\(fm)
.DE
.LP
\f[I]Step 2 (unconditioning).\f[R] The conditional mean $C sub M$ does not depend on $N$, so
.DS
	$Var ( Chat ) = Var ( C sub M ) + Ex left [ C sub M (1 - C sub M ) { n - N } over { N (n-1) } right ] + Ex left [ { (1 - C sub M ) eps } over { (1 - eps ) N } right ] .$	(A3)
.DE
$N$ is independent of $M$ and is $roman Bin (n, s)$ conditioned on $N >= 1$, so
$Ex [ 1/N ] = (ns) sup -1 (1 + O((ns) sup -1 ))$ and $Ex [ (n-N)/(N(n-1)) ] = (1-s)(ns) sup -1 (1 + O((ns) sup -1 ))$.
Over $M$, $Ex [ C sub M (1 - C sub M ) ] = theta (1 - theta ) - Var ( C sub M )$ and $Ex [ 1 - C sub M ] = 1 - theta$.
Substituting, and using $Var ( C sub M ) = Var ( N sub mut )/ n sup 2$, gives (7).
The term $- Var ( C sub M )(1-s)/(ns)$ is $O(1/(n cdot ns))$ and is absorbed into the remainder.
Unbiasedness (6) follows from $Ex [ C sub M ] = theta$. \(sq
.LP
\f[I]Remark.\f[R] Without the conditioning on $N >= 1$ (that is, setting $Chat = 0$ for an empty sketch),
$Ex [ Chat ] = theta (1 - (1-s) sup n )$, which matches the known FMH correction factor.
.HA B "Bloom-filter cardinality variance (Proposition 2c)"
.LP
Insert $n$ items with $h$ hashes into $m$ bits, and let $X$ be the number of zero bits. Classical occupancy
results (the linear-counting analysis [44]) give $Ex [X] approx m e sup -a$ and
$Var (X) approx m e sup -a (1 - (1+a) e sup -a )$ with $a = hn/m$.
The estimator $n hat = - (m/h) ln (X/m)$ then has, by the delta method,
.DS
	$Var ( n hat ) ~ approx ~ m over { h sup 2 } ( e sup a - 1 - a ) .$	(B1)
.DE
At the optimal load $a = ln 2$, with $m = c n$, $h = c ln 2$ and $c = log sub 2 (1/ eps ) / ln 2$, this becomes
.DS
	$Var ( n hat ) ~ approx ~ 0.443 ~ n / log sub 2 (1/ eps ) .$	(B2)
.DE
.LP
For BF\(caBF inclusion\(enexclusion, $| A \(ca B | hat = | A | + | B | - n hat ( A \(cu B )$, where $|A|$ and $|B|$ can be
stored exactly and $n hat ( A \(cu B )$ comes from the bitwise OR of the filters. Its variance is therefore
that of $n hat$ at $n = | A \(cu B | approx n sub B$, which is dominated by the larger set.
For Proposition 2(b): each of the $s sub 2 L sub B$ probes of non-members is an independent Bernoulli($eps$),
which gives mean $s sub 2 L sub B eps$ and variance $s sub 2 L sub B eps (1 - eps )$. \(sq
